INT. NIGHT FERRY "ADELAIDE" - GALLEY - NIGHT

A tiny steel kitchen. A porthole shows black water. Pans hang on a rail and sway with the swell.

ARASH, 60, cook, in a white apron, rolls dough on a floured board. CLEMENTINE, 30s, in a navy coat, sits on a stool by the oven. ABEL, 30s, in shirtsleeves, leans in the doorway with a paper cup of tea.

ARASH
You two can talk. I'm deaf in the oven ear.

CLEMENTINE
You don't have an oven ear.

ARASH
I have a lot of ears.

ABEL
Clem.

CLEMENTINE
Abel.

ABEL
Thirty-one days.

CLEMENTINE
Thirty-two. It's after midnight.

ABEL
Thirty-two, then.

CLEMENTINE
I wrote you.

ABEL
You wrote me once.

CLEMENTINE
I wrote you a very good one.

ABEL
It was good. It said you'd be somewhere else. It didn't say where.

CLEMENTINE
I didn't know where.

ABEL
You're on a ferry.

CLEMENTINE
It was where the ferry was going.

Arash turns the dough, folds it, turns it again. The ferry rolls. The pans clang softly.

ABEL
You could have called.

CLEMENTINE
So could you.

ABEL
I did. Four times.

CLEMENTINE
I know.

ABEL
You knew.

CLEMENTINE
I listened to every one.

ABEL
And?

CLEMENTINE
And I didn't pick up.

ABEL
Why not?

CLEMENTINE
Because you'd have asked me to come back, and I'd have said yes.

A silence. The oven ticks. Arash's hands slow on the dough.

ABEL
And that's bad.

CLEMENTINE
It was then.

ABEL
And now?

CLEMENTINE
Now I'd ask you to get on the ferry.

ABEL
I am on the ferry.

CLEMENTINE
I know. I saw you at the dock.

ABEL
You saw me.

CLEMENTINE
Thirty-two days. I know your walk from a quarter mile.

Abel sets down the tea. Arash, without turning, sets down the rolling pin. He wipes his hands, takes his coat from the hook, and goes out. The door swings shut behind him.

ABEL
Is it too late for the thing I wrote?

CLEMENTINE
Which thing?

ABEL
The reply.

CLEMENTINE
You never sent a reply.

ABEL
I wrote one every night.

He pulls a bundle of letters from his jacket, tied with kitchen twine. She stares at it, then takes it. Her thumb passes across the top envelope.

CLEMENTINE
(quiet)
Read me the first.

ABEL
It just says your name.

She nods. He begins to say it.
